WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.380
A bacterium senses most of its surroundings with two-component systems.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.360
Component one is a sensor kinase, usually set in the membrane.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.120
When it detects its signal, it takes the end phosphate from ATP.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.920
It puts that phosphate on one of its own histidines.

5
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.730
Component two is the responder.

6
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.990
It moves that phosphate onto one of its own aspartates.

7
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.990
The change in shape that follows switches it on.

8
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:41.030
Most responders are transcription factors, so the whole pathway is a sensor and a

9
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.410
gene switch with one phosphate passed between them.

10
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:47.893
Set this beside an animal pathway.

11
00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:51.433
A phosphate on aspartate is labile.

12
00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:58.293
How long it lasts differs from one responder to another, from seconds for CheY, a

13
00:00:58.293 --> 00:01:01.413
swimming responder, to minutes or more for others.

14
00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:08.613
A phosphate on serine, threonine or tyrosine stays until a phosphatase removes it.

15
00:01:09.013 --> 00:01:12.213
So the cell controls off separately from on.

16
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:17.133
Because that mark stays, another protein can bind it.

17
00:01:17.693 --> 00:01:21.073
An SH2 domain reads a phosphotyrosine.

18
00:01:22.073 --> 00:01:27.173
In the bacterial relay, the phosphate changes the responder that carries it, and no

19
00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:28.813
separate domain reads it.

20
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:34.813
Most sensor kinases pass their phosphate to one partner and largely ignore the rest.

21
00:01:35.713 --> 00:01:41.733
An animal receptor recruits several pathways at once, and each runs through tiers of kinases.
